    {
      "statement": "HTTPX supports setting up HTTP proxies via the proxy parameter.",
      "relation": "supports",
      "quote": "HTTPX supports setting up [HTTP proxies](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proxy_server#Web_proxy_servers) via the `proxy` parameter to be passed on client initialization or top-level API functions like `httpx.get(..., proxy=...)`."
    },
    {
      "statement": "HTTPX supports routing HTTPS requests through a proxy.",
      "relation": "supports",
      "quote": "For more advanced use cases, pass a mounts `dict`. For example, to route HTTP and HTTPS requests to 2 different proxies, respectively located at `http://localhost:8030`, and `http://localhost:8031`, pass a `dict` of proxy URLs:"
    },
    {
      "statement": "HTTPX supports using a proxy for HTTPS requests via the 'https://' mount key.",
      "relation": "supports",
      "quote": "proxy_mounts = {\n    \"http://\": httpx.HTTPTransport(proxy=\"http://localhost:8030\"),\n    \"https://\": httpx.HTTPTransport(proxy=\"http://localhost:8031\"),\n}"
    },
    {
      "statement": "HTTPX supports HTTPS proxies by using the HTTP scheme for the proxy URL.",
      "relation": "supports",
      "quote": "In most cases, the proxy URL for the `https://` key _should_ use the `http://` scheme (that's not a typo!)."
    },
    {
      "statement": "HTTPX supports HTTPS proxies by establishing a TCP connection to the server via the proxy and performing the TLS handshake.",
      "relation": "supports",
      "quote": "This mechanism is how you can access websites that use HTTPS from an HTTP proxy (the client \"upgrades\" the connection to HTTPS by performing the TLS handshake with the server over the TCP connection provided by the proxy)."
